01:19:57.680 --> 01:20:01.680
with the hybrid personal brand

01:19:59.080 --> 01:20:03.640
methodology. He is the golden goose.

01:20:01.680 --> 01:20:05.400
This is how he's been able to become the

01:20:03.640 --> 01:20:06.800
richest man in the world. And his hybrid

01:20:05.400 --> 01:20:09.320
personal brand is the golden goose, but

01:20:06.800 --> 01:20:11.040
he lays golden eggs that he can sell, or

01:20:09.320 --> 01:20:13.280
he can just sell parts of the companies

01:20:11.040 --> 01:20:15.240
in order to fund his other endeavors.

01:20:13.280 --> 01:20:16.880
And this is the same exact way that you

01:20:15.240 --> 01:20:18.440
should do things on YouTube. And one

01:20:16.880 --> 01:20:20.760
thing I'll say here is a lot of these

01:20:18.440 --> 01:20:22.840
companies have absolutely nothing to do

01:20:20.760 --> 01:20:25.000
with each other. SpaceX is about going

01:20:22.840 --> 01:20:27.000
to Mars. PayPal is about efficient money

01:20:25.000 --> 01:20:28.840
transfer. SolarCity is obviously about

01:20:27.000 --> 01:20:30.840
solar power. Tesla is about electric

01:20:28.840 --> 01:20:32.960
cars. Zip2 is kind of like an early

01:20:30.840 --> 01:20:35.160
version of Google Maps. x.com or

01:20:32.960 --> 01:20:36.960
twitter.com is for efficient human

01:20:35.160 --> 01:20:39.480
communication. Hyperloop is a

01:20:36.960 --> 01:20:40.640
transportation technology. Neuralink is

01:20:39.480 --> 01:20:42.480
honestly a little bit creepy, but it's

01:20:40.640 --> 01:20:44.080
about basically integrating computers

01:20:42.480 --> 01:20:46.320
and software with the human brain. And

01:20:44.080 --> 01:20:47.720
it is actually amazing for people who

01:20:46.320 --> 01:20:48.920
are paralyzed for instance. You know,

01:20:47.720 --> 01:20:50.320
there's been people that are paralyzed

01:20:48.920 --> 01:20:51.720
that have been able to play chess online

01:20:50.320 --> 01:20:53.840
for instance. The Boring Company is

01:20:51.720 --> 01:20:55.760
about boring holes underground for more

01:20:53.840 --> 01:20:57.680
efficient travel. And OpenAI is

01:20:55.760 --> 01:20:59.560
obviously about AI. And that's not even

01:20:57.680 --> 01:21:02.960
all of his companies. So, Elon Musk

01:20:59.560 --> 01:21:05.800
isn't just the AI guy or the SpaceX guy

01:21:02.960 --> 01:21:07.840
or the Tesla guy. He has a hybrid

01:21:05.800 --> 01:21:10.120
personal brand. He is a growing,

01:21:07.840 --> 01:21:12.040
changing human being who can launch

01:21:10.120 --> 01:21:14.240
different companies from his personal

01:21:12.040 --> 01:21:16.120
brand, right? He's the golden goose and

01:21:14.240 --> 01:21:18.320
the companies are the golden eggs. And

01:21:16.120 --> 01:21:20.680
hybrid personal brands are both specific

01:21:18.320 --> 01:21:22.360
and non-specific at the same time. You

01:21:20.680 --> 01:21:23.960
can pick a niche and I recommend that

01:21:22.360 --> 01:21:26.160
you pick a niche when you first start

01:21:23.960 --> 01:21:27.840
and then later you can expand and jump

01:21:26.160 --> 01:21:29.520
to another niche or you can become

01:21:27.840 --> 01:21:31.160
nicheless as your channel and your

01:21:29.520 --> 01:21:32.520
personal brand grows. And you can

01:21:31.160 --> 01:21:34.080
actually start a bunch of different

01:21:32.520 --> 01:21:35.960
companies. And there's very little

01:21:34.080 --> 01:21:38.280
downside to creating a hybrid personal

01:21:35.960 --> 01:21:39.320
brand and there's a ton of upside,

01:21:38.280 --> 01:21:42.000
right? So, you're not stuck in one

01:21:39.320 --> 01:21:43.960
niche. So, SEO for instance, if it stops

01:21:42.000 --> 01:21:45.760
becoming viable, you can very easily

01:21:43.960 --> 01:21:47.360
pivot to something else. So, you're

01:21:45.760 --> 01:21:49.200
probably thinking, "How do I apply this

01:21:47.360 --> 01:21:51.240
with my brand or business?" Well, first

01:21:49.200 --> 01:21:53.320
of all, you should be yourself, but you

01:21:51.240 --> 01:21:54.760
should be the best version of yourself.

01:21:53.320 --> 01:21:57.480
And there's eight what I like to call

01:21:54.760 --> 01:21:58.920
personality dials that make you unique.

01:21:57.480 --> 01:22:00.520
Now, the first one is your channel name

01:21:58.920 --> 01:22:02.120
and branding. So, you can use your real

01:22:00.520 --> 01:22:03.680
name, of course. You can also use your

01:22:02.120 --> 01:22:05.040
first name and then something else. For

01:22:03.680 --> 01:22:06.640
instance, one of my clients is called

01:22:05.040 --> 01:22:08.000
Felix and Friends. You could also use

01:22:06.640 --> 01:22:10.040
your first name and then your last name

01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:11.360
could be a pseudonym or a nickname. For

01:22:10.040 --> 01:22:13.320
instance, my name is actually Shane

01:22:11.360 --> 01:22:14.800
Hummuson, but my personal brand name is

01:22:13.320 --> 01:22:16.680
Shane Hummus. You could also just

01:22:14.800 --> 01:22:18.000
completely use a pseudonym. Just as an

01:22:16.680 --> 01:22:19.960
example of that, one of the biggest

01:22:18.000 --> 01:22:21.640
finance YouTubers is Tom Nash. That's

01:22:19.960 --> 01:22:23.200
not his real name. He wants to remain

01:22:21.640 --> 01:22:24.880
anonymous. Now, another thing you can do

01:22:23.200 --> 01:22:26.160
and I recommend being very careful with

01:22:24.880 --> 01:22:28.520
this, but it can be an effective

01:22:26.160 --> 01:22:30.360
strategy, is have your name and then put

01:22:28.520 --> 01:22:32.440
your niche in the channel name as well.

01:22:30.360 --> 01:22:34.480
Just as an example, Marko WhiteBoard

01:22:32.440 --> 01:22:35.880
Finance. He's got his first name, Marko,

01:22:34.480 --> 01:22:37.720
and then he talks about WhiteBoard

01:22:35.880 --> 01:22:39.440
Finance. Now, if you do this, you need

01:22:37.720 --> 01:22:40.960
to be very careful, right? You don't

01:22:39.440 --> 01:22:41.960
want to get too specific because again,

01:22:40.960 --> 01:22:43.760
you're going to be making that same

01:22:41.960 --> 01:22:45.160
mistake of not being able to pivot. And

01:22:43.760 --> 01:22:46.320
I do think that YouTube limits the

01:22:45.160 --> 01:22:48.040
amount of times that you can change your

01:22:46.320 --> 01:22:49.400
name. Second is positioning. Now,

01:22:48.040 --> 01:22:50.560
there's basically three main types of

01:22:49.400 --> 01:22:52.440
positioning and then there's a fourth

01:22:50.560 --> 01:22:54.640
type that I recommend. You can position

01:22:52.440 --> 01:22:56.040
yourself as a learner, an enthusiast,

01:22:54.640 --> 01:22:58.240
and an expert. Now, I'm going to use

01:22:56.040 --> 01:22:59.600
chess as the example here because I like

01:22:58.240 --> 01:23:00.920
playing chess. I've got some chess

01:22:59.600 --> 01:23:03.440
pieces in the background here. An

01:23:00.920 --> 01:23:05.240
example of a learner is Cameron. She

01:23:03.440 --> 01:23:07.080
basically started a channel where she

01:23:05.240 --> 01:23:09.200
was first learning how to play chess and

01:23:07.080 --> 01:23:11.120
she documented her journey of getting to

01:23:09.200 --> 01:23:12.520
a pretty good level, 2,000. Now, at this

01:23:11.120 --> 01:23:13.920
point, she's probably higher than a

01:23:12.520 --> 01:23:16.080
learner, but the way she first

01:23:13.920 --> 01:23:17.720
positioned herself was as a learner. And

01:23:16.080 --> 01:23:19.640
this can be really refreshing because

01:23:17.720 --> 01:23:22.280
let's be honest, most people in the

01:23:19.640 --> 01:23:23.680
world in any given subject are learners,

01:23:22.280 --> 01:23:25.360
right? They're beginners. And if you're

01:23:23.680 --> 01:23:27.000
a three out of 10 in any given skill,

01:23:25.360 --> 01:23:28.600
you can teach a one out of 10 how to get

01:23:27.000 --> 01:23:30.760
to three out of 10. In fact, in many

01:23:28.600 --> 01:23:32.600
cases, three out of 10s are better at

01:23:30.760 --> 01:23:34.160
teaching one out of 10s than 10 out of

01:23:32.600 --> 01:23:35.920
10s would be because someone who's a

01:23:34.160 --> 01:23:38.000
super grandmaster like the guy on the

01:23:35.920 --> 01:23:40.000
right, Daniel Naroditsky, was a beginner

01:23:38.000 --> 01:23:41.280
when he was like four years old. So,

01:23:40.000 --> 01:23:42.880
it's been so long since he's been a

01:23:41.280 --> 01:23:45.000
beginner, that it would be hard for him

01:23:42.880 --> 01:23:46.720
to even relate. And it would be hard for

01:23:45.000 --> 01:23:48.320
other people to relate to him. Then you

01:23:46.720 --> 01:23:50.040
can also position yourself as an

01:23:48.320 --> 01:23:51.280
enthusiast. So, this is somebody who's

01:23:50.040 --> 01:23:53.160
pretty good, but they're not

01:23:51.280 --> 01:23:55.200
world-class. So, an example of this

01:23:53.160 --> 01:23:57.040
would be Gotham Chess. So, he's probably

01:23:55.200 --> 01:23:58.960
like a seven out of 10, right? He's not

01:23:57.040 --> 01:24:01.000
a grandmaster, he's not a professional

01:23:58.960 --> 01:24:03.000
chess player, but he is quite good. He's

01:24:01.000 --> 01:24:04.520
a very high-level amateur. And so, an

01:24:03.000 --> 01:24:06.440
enthusiast, let's say they're a seven

01:24:04.520 --> 01:24:08.200
out of 10, they can teach a three out of

01:24:06.440 --> 01:24:09.720
10 how to get to seven out of 10. And

01:24:08.200 --> 01:24:11.240
they can also teach a one out of 10 how

01:24:09.720 --> 01:24:12.480
to get to seven out of 10 as well. Now,

01:24:11.240 --> 01:24:14.640
to be honest with you, positioning

01:24:12.480 --> 01:24:16.920
yourself as an enthusiast can be a huge

01:24:14.640 --> 01:24:19.120
advantage because a lot of people try to

01:24:16.920 --> 01:24:21.320
position themselves as experts in many

01:24:19.120 --> 01:24:22.760
cases when they actually aren't experts.

01:24:21.320 --> 01:24:25.400
And not only can that be a little bit

01:24:22.760 --> 01:24:26.760
cringe, but it's also unrelatable. So,

01:24:25.400 --> 01:24:28.440
even if you are an expert, like let's

01:24:26.760 --> 01:24:30.360
just say you're a super grandmaster, the

01:24:28.440 --> 01:24:32.840
concepts that you talk about are so

01:24:30.360 --> 01:24:33.840
above the average person who plays chess

01:24:32.840 --> 01:24:35.040
that they're probably not going to be

01:24:33.840 --> 01:24:36.600
able to learn much from that. And

01:24:35.040 --> 01:24:39.040
oftentimes enthusiasts are actually

01:24:36.600 --> 01:24:40.840
significantly better at teaching newbies

01:24:39.040 --> 01:24:42.840
and intermediates than experts are. Then

01:24:40.840 --> 01:24:44.920
the third type is the expert. Now, the

01:24:42.840 --> 01:24:46.480
example here is Daniel Naroditsky. Now,

01:24:44.920 --> 01:24:48.200
in his case, he's actually quite good at

01:24:46.480 --> 01:24:49.520
teaching and he's definitely a 10 out of

01:24:48.200 --> 01:24:50.960
10. He's a pretty much as good as it

01:24:49.520 --> 01:24:53.320
gets at chess. Right? He's a super

01:24:50.960 --> 01:24:55.080
grandmaster, like 2700 plus rating. But,

01:24:53.320 --> 01:24:56.680
a lot of experts actually aren't that

01:24:55.080 --> 01:24:58.280
good at teaching beginners,

01:24:56.680 --> 01:25:00.200
intermediates, or even people who are

01:24:58.280 --> 01:25:01.880
pretty good, like enthusiasts. And plus,

01:25:00.200 --> 01:25:02.880
they're just intimidating, right? Like

01:25:01.880 --> 01:25:04.080
someone will start watching their

01:25:02.880 --> 01:25:05.760
channel and they'll just think, "Oh,

01:25:04.080 --> 01:25:07.240
this guy is way too good. I can't learn

01:25:05.760 --> 01:25:09.400
anything from him." And so, I think the

01:25:07.240 --> 01:25:11.640
perfect positioning, if you can do it,

01:25:09.400 --> 01:25:13.280
is an expert enthusiast hybrid, right?

01:25:11.640 --> 01:25:15.360
So, an example of this, in my opinion,

01:25:13.280 --> 01:25:17.240
is Nomad Capitalist. He's somebody who

01:25:15.360 --> 01:25:18.920
helps business owners optimize their tax

01:25:17.240 --> 01:25:20.480
strategy, oftentimes by moving their

01:25:18.920 --> 01:25:22.160
businesses to different countries. Now,

01:25:20.480 --> 01:25:24.280
a lot of the time he will make mass

01:25:22.160 --> 01:25:26.400
market content talking about the news or

01:25:24.280 --> 01:25:27.720
talking about some new law that dropped.

01:25:26.400 --> 01:25:29.640
And most of the people who watch that

01:25:27.720 --> 01:25:31.000
content are not seven- and eight-figure

01:25:29.640 --> 01:25:32.880
business owners, which is his target

01:25:31.000 --> 01:25:34.840
demographic. But, he makes his content

01:25:32.880 --> 01:25:36.680
in such a way where it's valuable to the

01:25:34.840 --> 01:25:38.640
general audience, so it's mass market,

01:25:36.680 --> 01:25:40.400
but it's also valuable to seven- and

01:25:38.640 --> 01:25:42.200
eight-figure business owners. And so, he

01:25:40.400 --> 01:25:43.840
positions himself as an expert

01:25:42.200 --> 01:25:46.040
enthusiast hybrid. So, you get the

01:25:43.840 --> 01:25:48.000
upside of getting a lot of views and you

01:25:46.040 --> 01:25:49.680
end up attracting more of your target

01:25:48.000 --> 01:25:51.160
demographic, even if it's just a small

01:25:49.680 --> 01:25:52.400
percentage of your audience. Now, I'll

01:25:51.160 --> 01:25:54.080
do another example of learner that's

01:25:52.400 --> 01:25:56.640
outside of chess. There's this channel

01:25:54.080 --> 01:25:58.520
Moan who basically posted his first-ever

01:25:56.640 --> 01:26:00.560
time cold calling. And this video got

01:25:58.520 --> 01:26:03.080
over 300,000 views. And this was

01:26:00.560 --> 01:26:05.240
literally him just doing a thing that he

01:26:03.080 --> 01:26:06.640
was already going to do, right? He was

01:26:05.240 --> 01:26:08.600
cold calling businesses trying to get

01:26:06.640 --> 01:26:09.880
his first client, but he just recorded

01:26:08.600 --> 01:26:12.240
it. Like it's that easy. Like the

01:26:09.880 --> 01:26:14.760
content was unbelievably easy and he got

01:26:12.240 --> 01:26:16.200
over 300,000 views from it. Absolutely

01:26:14.760 --> 01:26:17.960
brilliant. An example of an enthusiast

01:26:16.200 --> 01:26:19.720
would be Ali Abdaal. He doesn't really

01:26:17.960 --> 01:26:21.400
position himself as an expert in

01:26:19.720 --> 01:26:23.560
anything. He positions himself as an

01:26:21.400 --> 01:26:25.120
enthusiast and then he talks about other

01:26:23.560 --> 01:26:27.040
experts. For instance, he talks about

01:26:25.120 --> 01:26:29.880
people who wrote books or he might talk

01:26:27.040 --> 01:26:32.200
about studies or numbers or statistics,

01:26:29.880 --> 01:26:34.360
etc. And then an expert would be someone

01:26:32.200 --> 01:26:36.040
like Cole Gordon who with all of his

01:26:34.360 --> 01:26:38.640
businesses at one point was making over

01:26:36.040 --> 01:26:40.880
$10 million a month. But he talks about

01:26:38.640 --> 01:26:42.920
really high-level stuff that almost only

01:26:40.880 --> 01:26:44.520
business owners would care about. And he

01:26:42.920 --> 01:26:46.720
doesn't get that many views. Now, an

01:26:44.520 --> 01:26:49.640
example of an expert enthusiast hybrid

01:26:46.720 --> 01:26:51.400
would be Alex Hormozi. He also attracts

01:26:49.640 --> 01:26:53.200
a lot of business owners, but he

01:26:51.400 --> 01:26:54.480
attracts people who are on a lower level

01:26:53.200 --> 01:26:55.640
as well and are just trying to learn

01:26:54.480 --> 01:26:57.440
business.

01:26:55.640 --> 01:26:59.160
So, he makes his content in such a way

01:26:57.440 --> 01:27:00.320
where it's attractive to a mass market

01:26:59.160 --> 01:27:02.480
audience,

01:27:00.320 --> 01:27:04.000
but it also attracts the right people

01:27:02.480 --> 01:27:06.000
that he wants to attract in order to

01:27:04.000 --> 01:27:07.760
sell his products and services to. So,

01:27:06.000 --> 01:27:09.760
that is positioning and positioning is

01:27:07.760 --> 01:27:11.520
extremely important. And by the way, you

01:27:09.760 --> 01:27:13.320
can change your positioning over time.

01:27:11.520 --> 01:27:15.680
You can start off as a learner and then

01:27:13.320 --> 01:27:17.520
shift to an enthusiast and then shift to

01:27:15.680 --> 01:27:19.920
an expert or an expert enthusiast

01:27:17.520 --> 01:27:21.680
hybrid. And oftentimes this strategy of

01:27:19.920 --> 01:27:23.400
building in public is extremely

01:27:21.680 --> 01:27:25.760
relatable and you'll build an audience

01:27:23.400 --> 01:27:28.040
that kind of wants to follow along with

01:27:25.760 --> 01:27:29.760
your journey and see your story unfold,

01:27:28.040 --> 01:27:31.640
which is really cool. And you can also

01:27:29.760 --> 01:27:33.480
position yourself differently in the

01:27:31.640 --> 01:27:35.200
different topics that you talk about.

01:27:33.480 --> 01:27:37.240
So, for instance, I'm an expert on

01:27:35.200 --> 01:27:39.360
YouTube, but I'm not an expert on paid

01:27:37.240 --> 01:27:41.000
ads. However, I'm getting into paid ads

01:27:39.360 --> 01:27:42.280
and I do talk about it in my content.

01:27:41.000 --> 01:27:44.120
So, I'm probably somewhere between like

01:27:42.280 --> 01:27:45.840
a learner and an enthusiast now when it

01:27:44.120 --> 01:27:47.320
comes to paid ads. And I'm an expert

01:27:45.840 --> 01:27:49.240
enthusiast when it comes to YouTube,

01:27:47.320 --> 01:27:50.720
personal branding, and monetizing a

01:27:49.240 --> 01:27:52.280
YouTube channel properly. Now, the next

01:27:50.720 --> 01:27:53.920
one is taxonomy and I'm not going to go

01:27:52.280 --> 01:27:56.000
into detail on this one, but this is

01:27:53.920 --> 01:27:57.160
basically the format of the videos that

01:27:56.000 --> 01:27:59.400
you make. So, for instance, you could

01:27:57.160 --> 01:28:01.720
make listical videos, how-to videos,

01:27:59.400 --> 01:28:03.160
case study videos, challenge videos,

01:28:01.720 --> 01:28:04.560
etc. And that's something that I'm going

01:28:03.160 --> 01:28:05.680
to cover in a different video on the

01:28:04.560 --> 01:28:07.680
channel. But next, we're going to talk

01:28:05.680 --> 01:28:09.880
about high-effort versus low-effort

01:28:07.680 --> 01:28:11.840
content. So, higher production does not

01:28:09.880 --> 01:28:13.720
always mean better results. And by the

01:28:11.840 --> 01:28:15.440
way, low-effort does not mean low

01:28:13.720 --> 01:28:17.200
quality. In fact, one of the main

01:28:15.440 --> 01:28:18.960
reasons that people watch YouTube when

01:28:17.200 --> 01:28:20.800
they could be watching Netflix or they

01:28:18.960 --> 01:28:22.480
could be watching TV, which both of

01:28:20.800 --> 01:28:24.840
those have extremely high production

01:28:22.480 --> 01:28:26.720
quality, is because YouTube feels much

01:28:24.840 --> 01:28:28.000
more authentic and relatable. Right?

01:28:26.720 --> 01:28:29.880
They want to feel almost like they're

01:28:28.000 --> 01:28:31.640
interacting with a real human being. And

01:28:29.880 --> 01:28:33.360
so, you can upload content that doesn't

01:28:31.640 --> 01:28:35.760
have a lot of edits and it's relatively

01:28:33.360 --> 01:28:37.560
basic, and as long as the actual quality

01:28:35.760 --> 01:28:38.880
of the content itself, aka the words

01:28:37.560 --> 01:28:40.400
that you say and the quality of the

01:28:38.880 --> 01:28:42.280
ideas and the concepts that you talk

01:28:40.400 --> 01:28:44.160
about are good, then it's still

01:28:42.280 --> 01:28:45.880
high-quality content, even though you

01:28:44.160 --> 01:28:47.440
didn't put that much effort into it. So,

01:28:45.880 --> 01:28:49.080
you could literally just share your

01:28:47.440 --> 01:28:50.720
journey and share the things that you're

01:28:49.080 --> 01:28:53.040
learning, and even though that's low

01:28:50.720 --> 01:28:54.720
effort content, it's still high-quality

01:28:53.040 --> 01:28:55.920
if it's valuable to other people. Now,

01:28:54.720 --> 01:28:57.920
by the way, I recommend when it comes to

01:28:55.920 --> 01:28:59.880
the format, start off really simple,

01:28:57.920 --> 01:29:01.400
stick with listicles, simple explainer

01:28:59.880 --> 01:29:02.840
videos, and straightforward how-to

01:29:01.400 --> 01:29:04.920
videos. And then you can also do a case

01:29:02.840 --> 01:29:06.240
study on yourself and/or your clients.

01:29:04.920 --> 01:29:08.000
Now, the big thing here is the idea

01:29:06.240 --> 01:29:09.040
matters much more than the production

01:29:08.000 --> 01:29:10.560
quality. And this is something that I

01:29:09.040 --> 01:29:12.560
talk about all the time on the channel,

01:29:10.560 --> 01:29:14.400
the way you find the right ideas to talk

01:29:12.560 --> 01:29:16.720
about on YouTube or really any other

01:29:14.400 --> 01:29:18.040
social media platform is with the ICON

01:29:16.720 --> 01:29:19.440
method. So, check that out if you

01:29:18.040 --> 01:29:21.200
haven't already. And the whole purpose

01:29:19.440 --> 01:29:22.600
here is to keep your content to the

01:29:21.200 --> 01:29:24.320
point where you don't have to put a

01:29:22.600 --> 01:29:26.840
tremendous amount of effort into it, so

01:29:24.320 --> 01:29:29.240
that you can test more ideas faster to

01:29:26.840 --> 01:29:31.240
find what works. Then, when you find

01:29:29.240 --> 01:29:33.240
what works, you can increase your

01:29:31.240 --> 01:29:35.080
content quality or the production

01:29:33.240 --> 01:29:36.880
quality of your content, aka you can

01:29:35.080 --> 01:29:38.360
edit more, you can put more time into

01:29:36.880 --> 01:29:40.120
the video, and you can basically

01:29:38.360 --> 01:29:42.280
optimize it. Next, let's talk about

01:29:40.120 --> 01:29:43.840
passion versus practicality. And this is

01:29:42.280 --> 01:29:45.880
something that's going to be different

01:29:43.840 --> 01:29:48.080
for everyone. So, this really just comes

01:29:45.880 --> 01:29:49.920
down to your values and what you want to

01:29:48.080 --> 01:29:51.480
get out of life. If you really want to

01:29:49.920 --> 01:29:54.080
take this seriously and treat it like a

01:29:51.480 --> 01:29:56.520
business, you need to hyperfocus on what

01:29:54.080 --> 01:29:58.360
your viewers and customers want. And if

01:29:56.520 --> 01:30:00.440
you want this to be more of a lifestyle

01:29:58.360 --> 01:30:02.360
business, then you want to hyperfocus on

01:30:00.440 --> 01:30:04.280
what you want. But realistically

01:30:02.360 --> 01:30:06.200
speaking, almost everyone is going to

01:30:04.280 --> 01:30:07.840
lie somewhere in between these two

01:30:06.200 --> 01:30:09.280
spectrums. And my best advice for

01:30:07.840 --> 01:30:10.640
finding the perfect balance is what's

01:30:09.280 --> 01:30:12.680
known as ikigai. And this is the

01:30:10.640 --> 01:30:14.640
Japanese secret to a long and happy

01:30:12.680 --> 01:30:15.760
life. Now, ikigai gets a little more

01:30:14.640 --> 01:30:17.120
complicated than this, you know,

01:30:15.760 --> 01:30:18.840
treating it like a business versus

01:30:17.120 --> 01:30:20.960
treating it kind of like a hobby. They

01:30:18.840 --> 01:30:23.200
break it down into what you love, what

01:30:20.960 --> 01:30:25.040
the world needs, what you can be paid

01:30:23.200 --> 01:30:26.960
for, and what you're good at. Now,

01:30:25.040 --> 01:30:28.720
generally speaking, what the world needs

01:30:26.960 --> 01:30:30.480
and what you can be paid for are usually

01:30:28.720 --> 01:30:32.200
the same thing, but not always. And what

01:30:30.480 --> 01:30:34.000
you're good at is basically your natural

01:30:32.200 --> 01:30:35.760
talents or something that you spent a

01:30:34.000 --> 01:30:37.360
lot of time doing. Then of course, what

01:30:35.760 --> 01:30:38.560
you love is pretty self-explanatory.

01:30:37.360 --> 01:30:40.040
It's what you actually enjoy talking

01:30:38.560 --> 01:30:42.040
about. Now, you want to try to get as

01:30:40.040 --> 01:30:43.520
close to the middle as possible. Because

01:30:42.040 --> 01:30:45.120
if you find something that you love and

01:30:43.520 --> 01:30:46.680
it's something that the world needs, but

01:30:45.120 --> 01:30:47.920
it doesn't have the other two, then it's

01:30:46.680 --> 01:30:49.240
a mission. If you find something that

01:30:47.920 --> 01:30:50.600
you love and it's something that you're

01:30:49.240 --> 01:30:52.320
good at, but it doesn't have the other

01:30:50.600 --> 01:30:53.520
two, then it's a passion. If you find

01:30:52.320 --> 01:30:55.200
something that you're good at and it's

01:30:53.520 --> 01:30:56.480
something you can be paid for, but it

01:30:55.200 --> 01:30:57.880
doesn't have the other two, then it's a

01:30:56.480 --> 01:30:59.240
profession. If you find something that

01:30:57.880 --> 01:31:00.280
you can be paid for and it's something

01:30:59.240 --> 01:31:01.480
that the world needs, but it doesn't

01:31:00.280 --> 01:31:03.240
have the other two, then it's a

01:31:01.480 --> 01:31:05.000
vocation. And you want to try to get as

01:31:03.240 --> 01:31:05.800
close to the middle as possible. I'm

01:31:05.000 --> 01:31:07.080
going to be honest with you, when it

01:31:05.800 --> 01:31:09.240
comes to starting a business, the one

01:31:07.080 --> 01:31:11.480
that matters the most is what the world

01:31:09.240 --> 01:31:13.320
needs or what you can be paid for. Now,

01:31:11.480 --> 01:31:15.120
usually most successful personal brand

01:31:13.320 --> 01:31:16.800
YouTubers, business owners, etc., they

01:31:15.120 --> 01:31:18.240
start off with what the world needs and

01:31:16.800 --> 01:31:20.920
what you can be paid for, and then

01:31:18.240 --> 01:31:22.680
slowly over time, they kind of slide

01:31:20.920 --> 01:31:24.680
over to what they love or what they're

01:31:22.680 --> 01:31:26.600
good at, etc. But the important thing is

01:31:24.680 --> 01:31:28.520
is that you establish an audience.

01:31:26.600 --> 01:31:29.880
You're giving value to people and then

01:31:28.520 --> 01:31:31.600
you can kind of pivot a little bit more

01:31:29.880 --> 01:31:33.320
to something that you love doing more.

01:31:31.600 --> 01:31:34.680
That is my recommendation here. But

01:31:33.320 --> 01:31:36.480
ideally, you always want to strive to

01:31:34.680 --> 01:31:37.600
get to the middle and find your ikigai.

01:31:36.480 --> 01:31:39.080
And if you want to know more on this, I

01:31:37.600 --> 01:31:40.520
highly recommend reading the book. It's

01:31:39.080 --> 01:31:43.040
a great read. All right, so now let's

01:31:40.520 --> 01:31:45.040
talk about broad versus niche topics.

01:31:43.040 --> 01:31:47.160
Because most people make content that's

01:31:45.040 --> 01:31:50.040
too broad too soon. And what you want to

01:31:47.160 --> 01:31:52.400
do instead is start as the biggest fish

01:31:50.040 --> 01:31:54.080
in a puddle, not a small fish in the

01:31:52.400 --> 01:31:56.000
ocean. So, just as an example, when I

01:31:54.080 --> 01:31:58.160
first started as a career coach, I was

01:31:56.000 --> 01:32:01.360
helping pharmacy school students not get

01:31:58.160 --> 01:32:03.440
jobs, but just pass a very specific

01:32:01.360 --> 01:32:05.600
pharmacy school test called the PCAT.

01:32:03.440 --> 01:32:07.840
That's how niche I was. And then I

01:32:05.600 --> 01:32:09.320
expanded to helping pharmacy students in

01:32:07.840 --> 01:32:10.840
general, and then helping pharmacy

01:32:09.320 --> 01:32:12.800
students get jobs, and then helping

01:32:10.840 --> 01:32:14.480
pharmacy students get remote jobs, and

01:32:12.800 --> 01:32:16.240
then helping people get jobs in general,

01:32:14.480 --> 01:32:17.760
and then helping people get remote jobs,

01:32:16.240 --> 01:32:19.520
and now I can basically talk about any

01:32:17.760 --> 01:32:21.320
topic that I want. But it's important to

01:32:19.520 --> 01:32:23.520
note here that I started off as the

01:32:21.320 --> 01:32:25.120
biggest fish in a puddle. I did not go

01:32:23.520 --> 01:32:27.800
straight to the ocean. So you need to

01:32:25.120 --> 01:32:29.120
start niche and then expand as you grow.

01:32:27.800 --> 01:32:31.400
This is exactly what all of your

01:32:29.120 --> 01:32:33.720
favorite YouTubers do. Now some of them

01:32:31.400 --> 01:32:35.240
leave their old videos up on YouTube,

01:32:33.720 --> 01:32:36.720
and then some of them make them private

01:32:35.240 --> 01:32:38.840
or they delete them. But this is what

01:32:36.720 --> 01:32:40.760
basically all of them did. They started

01:32:38.840 --> 01:32:42.480
off very niche. Alex Hormozi for

01:32:40.760 --> 01:32:45.120
instance, when he first started, he was

01:32:42.480 --> 01:32:47.480
only talking about sales for gym

01:32:45.120 --> 01:32:49.560
businesses, right? Getting sales as a

01:32:47.480 --> 01:32:51.320
gym business. And then he expanded to

01:32:49.560 --> 01:32:52.440
everything for gym businesses, and then

01:32:51.320 --> 01:32:54.160
he expanded to sales for

01:32:52.440 --> 01:32:55.880
brick-and-mortar businesses, and then he

01:32:54.160 --> 01:32:57.360
expanded to sales in general, and then

01:32:55.880 --> 01:32:58.600
he added on marketing, and then he

01:32:57.360 --> 01:33:00.360
basically talks about everything for

01:32:58.600 --> 01:33:02.400
businesses. And he even started talking

01:33:00.360 --> 01:33:04.320
about dieting and like how to get cheap

01:33:02.400 --> 01:33:06.880
protein and stuff like that, right? So

01:33:04.320 --> 01:33:08.560
you see my point. Now he's a big fish,

01:33:06.880 --> 01:33:10.080
or really he's like a whale that's in

01:33:08.560 --> 01:33:11.880
the ocean. But he started off as the

01:33:10.080 --> 01:33:14.320
biggest fish in a puddle, and then he

01:33:11.880 --> 01:33:15.960
expanded. So the evolution path here is

01:33:14.320 --> 01:33:17.680
be the biggest fish in a puddle, conquer

01:33:15.960 --> 01:33:19.280
that puddle, then be the biggest fish in

01:33:17.680 --> 01:33:21.920
a pond, then be the biggest fish in a

01:33:19.280 --> 01:33:23.400
pool, then a lake, then a river, then an

01:33:21.920 --> 01:33:25.400
ocean. Now I am going to talk about this

01:33:23.400 --> 01:33:27.520
in other videos, but while you're doing

01:33:25.400 --> 01:33:29.480
all of this, make sure to tell your

01:33:27.520 --> 01:33:31.440
story. Make sure to showcase your

01:33:29.480 --> 01:33:32.880
personality, because that is what is

01:33:31.440 --> 01:33:34.760
going to get people to see you as a

01:33:32.880 --> 01:33:37.200
human being and see you as a friend, so

01:33:34.760 --> 01:33:39.280
that when you change topics, they will

01:33:37.200 --> 01:33:41.320
follow you to the new topics. Now a

01:33:39.280 --> 01:33:44.440
really good analogy for this is let's

01:33:41.320 --> 01:33:46.240
just pretend that you met somebody at a

01:33:44.440 --> 01:33:48.160
photography class, right? And the type

01:33:46.240 --> 01:33:50.240
of photography class it was was a

01:33:48.160 --> 01:33:51.560
portrait photography class. So you're

01:33:50.240 --> 01:33:52.960
both going to this portrait photography

01:33:51.560 --> 01:33:54.960
class, and this is the first time you've

01:33:52.960 --> 01:33:57.040
ever met each other. If you were to ask

01:33:54.960 --> 01:33:58.720
that person that you just met if they

01:33:57.040 --> 01:34:00.920
want to go on a weekend trip with you to

01:33:58.720 --> 01:34:02.200
Mexico, they are probably going to tell

01:34:00.920 --> 01:34:03.280
you no, right? They're going to make

01:34:02.200 --> 01:34:04.240
some kind of excuse and then they're

01:34:03.280 --> 01:34:06.120
going to text all their friends that

01:34:04.240 --> 01:34:08.200
they met a weirdo at photography class,

01:34:06.120 --> 01:34:10.480
right? However, if you were to tell that

01:34:08.200 --> 01:34:12.000
person, "Hey, we're both interested in

01:34:10.480 --> 01:34:13.840
portrait photography, do you want to

01:34:12.000 --> 01:34:15.760
meet up this weekend and take portrait

01:34:13.840 --> 01:34:17.480
photography photos?" There's a good

01:34:15.760 --> 01:34:18.680
chance that they're going to say yes.

01:34:17.480 --> 01:34:20.240
And so you do that, you take some

01:34:18.680 --> 01:34:21.760
portrait photography photos, and then a

01:34:20.240 --> 01:34:23.440
few weeks later you ask them again,

01:34:21.760 --> 01:34:25.160
"Hey, our portrait photography photos

01:34:23.440 --> 01:34:27.280
turned out great. Do you want to meet up

01:34:25.160 --> 01:34:29.360
and take some landscape photography

01:34:27.280 --> 01:34:30.360
photos?" There's a good chance that if

01:34:29.360 --> 01:34:32.560
they're interested in portrait

01:34:30.360 --> 01:34:34.120
photography, they're also interested in

01:34:32.560 --> 01:34:35.840
landscape photography. And there's a

01:34:34.120 --> 01:34:37.560
good chance that they would say yes to

01:34:35.840 --> 01:34:39.560
that as well. A few weeks later, you

01:34:37.560 --> 01:34:41.280
both come to the same class, and you ask

01:34:39.560 --> 01:34:43.400
them, "Hey, do you want to do some

01:34:41.280 --> 01:34:44.880
videography?" Right? Videography is

01:34:43.400 --> 01:34:46.480
probably something they're also

01:34:44.880 --> 01:34:47.600
interested in if they do photography.

01:34:46.480 --> 01:34:49.320
And there's a good chance they're going

01:34:47.600 --> 01:34:50.680
to say yes to that. And then a few weeks

01:34:49.320 --> 01:34:52.760
later you're like, "Hey, do you want to

01:34:50.680 --> 01:34:54.560
go to a painting class?" Well, they're

01:34:52.760 --> 01:34:56.120
already interested in photography, which

01:34:54.560 --> 01:34:57.920
is art, and so there's a good chance

01:34:56.120 --> 01:34:59.360
they'd also be interested in painting.

01:34:57.920 --> 01:35:00.920
And then after that, you just ask them,

01:34:59.360 --> 01:35:02.240
"Hey, do you want to go hang out and

01:35:00.920 --> 01:35:03.520
just talk about art?" There's a good

01:35:02.240 --> 01:35:05.240
chance they'd be interested in that. And

01:35:03.520 --> 01:35:06.800
then finally, a few months later, you

01:35:05.240 --> 01:35:08.560
ask them, "Hey, do you want to go on a

01:35:06.800 --> 01:35:10.280
weekend trip to Mexico?" There's a good

01:35:08.560 --> 01:35:11.960
chance they're going to say yes at that

01:35:10.280 --> 01:35:14.560
point. Do you see the point here? You

01:35:11.960 --> 01:35:16.000
started off very niche, you met this new

01:35:14.560 --> 01:35:18.160
person who's a stranger, which is kind

01:35:16.000 --> 01:35:20.320
of like people watching you on YouTube,

01:35:18.160 --> 01:35:23.080
and if the next video you uploaded was

01:35:20.320 --> 01:35:25.160
something completely unrelated, they're

01:35:23.080 --> 01:35:26.560
probably not going to watch it. they've

01:35:25.160 --> 01:35:28.200
watched a lot of your videos and they've

01:35:26.560 --> 01:35:30.280
got a lot of value from you, and they

01:35:28.200 --> 01:35:32.080
get to know you as a human being because

01:35:30.280 --> 01:35:33.800
you tell your story, and because you

01:35:32.080 --> 01:35:35.640
have a personality and you showcase it

01:35:33.800 --> 01:35:37.120
on your channel, then down the line,

01:35:35.640 --> 01:35:39.040
there's a very good chance that they'll

01:35:37.120 --> 01:35:40.920
watch whatever you put out. And that's

01:35:39.040 --> 01:35:42.840
exactly what happens with brands like

01:35:40.920 --> 01:35:44.760
Alex Hormozi, as well as brands like

01:35:42.840 --> 01:35:47.320
myself. Next, let's talk about the

01:35:44.760 --> 01:35:48.680
entertainment versus education ratio.

01:35:47.320 --> 01:35:50.560
Now, this one's really interesting

01:35:48.680 --> 01:35:52.560
because you want to decide here how much

01:35:50.560 --> 01:35:54.760
do you prioritize entertaining your

01:35:52.560 --> 01:35:56.840
audience versus educating them? Now,

01:35:54.760 --> 01:35:58.840
realistically speaking, you do not want

01:35:56.840 --> 01:36:00.360
your videos to be dead boring. But, at

01:35:58.840 --> 01:36:02.480
the same time, you don't want to come

01:36:00.360 --> 01:36:03.720
across like a dancing clown, right?

01:36:02.480 --> 01:36:04.960
Because then nobody's going to take you

01:36:03.720 --> 01:36:06.600
seriously, and they're not going to see

01:36:04.960 --> 01:36:07.880
you as an authority. And so, therefore,

01:36:06.600 --> 01:36:10.640
they're not going to buy your stuff.

01:36:07.880 --> 01:36:12.240
Now, generally speaking, people buy from

01:36:10.640 --> 01:36:14.240
education channels because you're

01:36:12.240 --> 01:36:15.280
solving real problems for them. And

01:36:14.240 --> 01:36:17.400
they're much more likely to buy

01:36:15.280 --> 01:36:18.920
higher-ticket products as well. Whereas,

01:36:17.400 --> 01:36:21.040
entertainment channels need to get a

01:36:18.920 --> 01:36:22.440
massive amount of views if they want to

01:36:21.040 --> 01:36:24.000
make money. Because people are way less

01:36:22.440 --> 01:36:25.040
likely to buy from them, and if they do

01:36:24.000 --> 01:36:26.840
buy, it's probably going to be

01:36:25.040 --> 01:36:27.920
lower-ticket products. Now, there's some

01:36:26.840 --> 01:36:30.360
channels that are heavy on

01:36:27.920 --> 01:36:32.200
entertainment, aka 60% entertainment,

01:36:30.360 --> 01:36:33.440
40% education. There's some channels

01:36:32.200 --> 01:36:36.120
that have a really good balance, like

01:36:33.440 --> 01:36:38.960
50/50. What I recommend is being about

01:36:36.120 --> 01:36:40.480
80% education and 20% entertainment.

01:36:38.960 --> 01:36:42.000
Right? So, you want to mainly educate

01:36:40.480 --> 01:36:43.760
them, and you do want the topics of your

01:36:42.000 --> 01:36:45.600
videos to be entertaining. But, at the

01:36:43.760 --> 01:36:47.080
same time, you want to be a little bit

01:36:45.600 --> 01:36:49.040
entertaining. Crack a joke once in a

01:36:47.080 --> 01:36:51.160
while, have a meme pop up on the screen

01:36:49.040 --> 01:36:52.960
once in a while. Use a lot of analogies

01:36:51.160 --> 01:36:54.520
and metaphors. Come up with a different

01:36:52.960 --> 01:36:56.400
ways of explaining things. Try to make

01:36:54.520 --> 01:36:57.960
it as visual as possible, and just go

01:36:56.400 --> 01:36:59.400
ahead and entertain them a little bit.

01:36:57.960 --> 01:37:01.040
Cuz it's going to make the whole process

01:36:59.400 --> 01:37:02.800
of learning a lot better. You know, when

01:37:01.040 --> 01:37:04.760
I think back on school, for instance, my

01:37:02.800 --> 01:37:06.880
favorite teachers were the ones that

01:37:04.760 --> 01:37:08.160
made topics interesting. Right? There

01:37:06.880 --> 01:37:10.000
might have been other teachers that were

01:37:08.160 --> 01:37:11.440
smarter than them or more educated than

01:37:10.000 --> 01:37:12.960
them, but some of those teachers were

01:37:11.440 --> 01:37:14.800
really boring. And the ones that

01:37:12.960 --> 01:37:16.920
actually did the best job educating

01:37:14.800 --> 01:37:18.480
people, paradoxically, were the ones

01:37:16.920 --> 01:37:20.040
that actually entertained them a little

01:37:18.480 --> 01:37:21.440
bit. But, of course, you can take this

01:37:20.040 --> 01:37:23.080
too far to the point where it's pretty

01:37:21.440 --> 01:37:24.240
much all entertainment, and then people

01:37:23.080 --> 01:37:25.480
are just going to see you as a dancing

01:37:24.240 --> 01:37:27.440
clown, and they're not going to take you

01:37:25.480 --> 01:37:30.360
seriously. So, this is a fine balance,

01:37:27.440 --> 01:37:31.600
and I recommend 80% education and 20%

01:37:30.360 --> 01:37:33.440
entertainment. Now, let's talk about

01:37:31.600 --> 01:37:35.800
controversial versus family-friendly.

01:37:33.440 --> 01:37:37.800
And this style determines how spicy your

